    "content": "Prime Minister Hamza Abdi Barre of Somalia's Federal Government met with Wamkele Mene, Secretary-General of the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), in Mogadishu on August 18, 2026. The meeting marked the first official visit by the AfCFTA chief to Somalia since the country's ratification of the trade agreement.\n\nDuring the talks, the two officials focused on how Somalia can leverage its membership in AfCFTA to expand market access for Somali products and services. The Prime Minister emphasized that the government is committed to translating trade agreements into concrete economic gains for Somali citizens, particularly by supporting local businesses and attracting foreign investment.\n\nThe visit follows Somalia's formal participation in AfCFTA, which the government has prioritized as part of its broader economic reform agenda. Analysts note that membership offers Somali firms new export opportunities across 54 African nations, but challenges such as weak infrastructure and regulatory gaps must be addressed to fully benefit.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, expanded free trade could lower costs for cross-border business and open new investment channels, particularly in sectors like livestock, agriculture, and telecommunications. The government has urged diaspora entrepreneurs to explore partnerships that leverage AfCFTA's tariff reductions and simplified customs procedures.\n\nThe meeting concluded with both sides expressing optimism about future collaboration, with plans for technical follow-ups to support Somalia's implementation of AfCFTA protocols.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/08/18/raiisul-wasaare-xamsa-oo-muqdisho-ku-qaabilay-xoghayaha-guud-ee-afcfta/",
