    "summary": "Wamkele Mene, head of the African Continental Free Trade Area, held talks with Somali labor leaders to discuss integrating workers into Africa's trade framework. The meeting focused on job creation and labor rights under Somalia's AfCFTA implementation.",
    "content": "The Secretary-General of the African Continental Free Trade Area (AfCFTA), Wamkele Mene, met with representatives of the Federation of Somali Trade Unions (FESTU) in Mogadishu on Monday. The delegation, led by FESTU General Secretary Omar Faruk Osman, discussed the role of organized labor in Somalia's economic integration under the continental trade pact. This dialogue is part of broader efforts to align national policies with AfCFTA objectives.\n\nSomalia ratified the AfCFTA agreement in 2019, committing to reduce tariffs and liberalize trade across the continent. However, implementation has been gradual, with challenges including a nascent private sector, weak infrastructure, and limited institutional capacity. The meeting underscores an intent to ensure that workers are central to trade reforms, a stance echoed by the AfCFTA secretariat which has consistently highlighted the need for inclusive growth.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the outcome of these discussions could hold significant implications. Many Somali businesses abroad operate in sectors that stand to benefit from reduced trade barriers, such as livestock, agriculture, and telecommunications. Furthermore, protections for workers and decent job creation are pressing concerns for diaspora members who invest in Somalia or consider returning. The engagement with trade unions signals a move toward more equitable trade policies that could foster sustainable employment.\n\nThe meeting in Mogadishu is part of a broader tour by Mene across African states to consult stakeholders on maximizing the benefits of the AfCFTA. While no concrete outcomes were announced, both sides expressed a commitment to ongoing cooperation, with plans for technical working groups to address labour standards and workforce skills development. As the continent moves toward fuller economic integration, the role of unions in shaping these policies will be a point of continued attention.\n\nRead the original report from Radio Dalsan here: https://radiodalsan.com/2026/08/18/afcfta-secretary-general-festu-discuss-greater-trade-union-role-in-africas-trade-and-economic-integration/?utm_source=rss&#038;utm_medium=rss&#038;utm_campaign=afcfta-secretary-general-festu-discuss-greater-trade-union-role-in-africas-trade-and-economic-integration",
