    "id": "news-1787044974417-1",
    "title": "Nairobi Hosts Final Talks on Agreement Between Ahmed Madobe and Abdiraashid Janan",
    "original_title": "Heshiiskii Axmed Madoobe &#038; Cabdirashiid Janan oo lagu dhammaystirayo Nairobi.",
    "slug": "nairobi-hosts-final-talks-on-agreement-between-ahmed-madobe-and-abdiraashid-janan",
    "summary": "Negotiations to finalize an agreement between Jubaland President Ahmed Madobe and former SNA commander Abdiraashid Janan begin in Nairobi, Kenya, today. The talks mark a step toward resolving a longstanding political and military dispute affecting Somalia's Jubaland region.",
    "content": "Negotiations aimed at finalizing an agreement between Ahmed Mohamed Islam, known as Ahmed Madobe, the President of Jubaland, and Abdiraashid Janan, a former commander of the Somali National Army, commenced today in Nairobi, Kenya. The talks are being held at an undisclosed location in the Kenyan capital, bringing together representatives from both sides to reach a conclusive pact.\n\nThe dispute between Madobe and Janan has been a source of tension in Jubaland, particularly in the Gedo region, where both have vested interests and influence. Janan, who previously served as the commander of the Somali National Army's 43rd division, has been a vocal critic of Madobe's administration, leading to sporadic clashes and political friction. The Nairobi negotiations follow several rounds of dialogue facilitated by international partners, including the African Union and the Somali federal government, which have pressured both leaders to resolve their differences peacefully.\n\nThe agreement under discussion is expected to address key issues such as security arrangements, power-sharing, and the reintegration of forces loyal to both factions into unified regional command structures. A successful outcome could stabilize Jubaland, which serves as a crucial buffer against extremist groups operating in the Horn of Africa.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this development holds particular significance. Many diaspora members trace their roots to Jubaland and Gedo, and prolonged instability has hindered their ability to invest, visit, or engage in rebuilding efforts. The finalization of this agreement could reopen avenues for economic cooperation and diaspora-driven development projects, which have often been stalled due to the volatile security situation.\n\nUntil the talks conclude, observers remain cautious, noting that similar attempts in the past have faltered due to mistrust and competing interests. However, today's engagement signals a continued commitment by regional actors to de-escalate tensions and prioritize dialogue over confrontation.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/heshiiskii-axmed-madoobe-cabdirashiid-janan-oo-lagu-dhammaystirayo-nairobi/424926/",
    "source": "horseedmedia.net",
